1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,951
In the summer of nineteen twenty, a New

2
00:00:03,991 --> 00:00:07,018
York front page called him the financial

3
00:00:07,058 --> 00:00:09,932
wizard. Nine million dollars, in a few

4
00:00:09,972 --> 00:00:12,796
months. From postage coupons.

5
00:00:13,846 --> 00:00:17,849
U.S. searches for mystery in Ponzi coup.

6
00:00:20,346 --> 00:00:22,642
New troubles are piling up for Charles

7
00:00:22,682 --> 00:00:25,102
Ponzi, the financial wizard, who is said

8
00:00:25,142 --> 00:00:27,254
to have made more than nine million

9
00:00:27,294 --> 00:00:29,590
dollars in a few months, by dealing in

10
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:32,013
international postal coupons.

11
00:00:32,663 --> 00:00:35,218
But the same story printed one more

12
00:00:35,258 --> 00:00:37,886
number, from the Boston post office.

13
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:40,555
It should have ended everything that

14
00:00:40,595 --> 00:00:43,595
afternoon. Instead, people kept lining up

15
00:00:43,635 --> 00:00:45,893
at his door for nine more days.

16
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:47,672
So what was that number?

17
00:00:47,712 --> 00:00:50,415
And how did one man keep a whole city

18
00:00:50,455 --> 00:00:51,796
believing?

19
00:00:52,546 --> 00:00:55,445
Part one. Fifty per cent.

20
00:00:56,795 --> 00:00:59,827
This is Charles Ponzi, in a Bain News

21
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:01,405
Service photograph.

22
00:01:01,445 --> 00:01:03,979
An Italian immigrant in Boston.

23
00:01:04,019 --> 00:01:06,969
Small, smiling, always well dressed.

24
00:01:07,009 --> 00:01:10,124
In nineteen twenty, his business had a

25
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:13,031
plain name. The Securities Exchange

26
00:01:13,071 --> 00:01:14,335
Company.

27
00:01:15,385 --> 00:01:18,385
The wire story reached Phoenix, Arizona

28
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:19,905
under this headline.

29
00:01:19,945 --> 00:01:22,792
Makes dollars gain fifty cents, within

30
00:01:22,832 --> 00:01:25,984
fifty four days. Even the headline had the

31
00:01:26,024 --> 00:01:29,176
days wrong. Further down the column, it is

32
00:01:29,216 --> 00:01:30,652
forty five.

33
00:01:31,702 --> 00:01:34,363
For several weeks, crowds have flocked to

34
00:01:34,403 --> 00:01:37,063
Ponzi's offices, and given their savings

35
00:01:37,103 --> 00:01:39,764
in exchange for notes of the company, for

36
00:01:39,804 --> 00:01:42,530
the principal plus fifty per cent, payable

37
00:01:42,570 --> 00:01:45,231
in ninety days. Invariably, Ponzi is said

38
00:01:45,271 --> 00:01:47,800
to have paid off the note in forty five

39
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,566
days. And there has been no complaint that

40
00:01:50,606 --> 00:01:53,069
any person has failed to receive money

41
00:01:53,109 --> 00:01:54,302
when due.

42
00:01:55,352 --> 00:01:57,806
No complaint. That was Ponzi's best

43
00:01:57,846 --> 00:02:00,584
argument. People put money in, and took

44
00:02:00,624 --> 00:02:03,292
more money out. But what was he buying

45
00:02:03,332 --> 00:02:04,502
with it?

46
00:02:05,552 --> 00:02:08,731
Ponzi describes his exchange system, as

47
00:02:08,771 --> 00:02:11,125
being based on the use of the

48
00:02:11,165 --> 00:02:14,261
international reply coupon, authorized

49
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:18,285
under the international postal agreement.

50
00:02:19,635 --> 00:02:22,511
A reply coupon was a small slip sold at

51
00:02:22,551 --> 00:02:24,753
post offices around the world.

52
00:02:24,793 --> 00:02:27,744
You mailed it abroad, and the person who

53
00:02:27,784 --> 00:02:30,884
got it could trade it for a stamp at their

54
00:02:30,924 --> 00:02:34,024
own post office. Ponzi's story was simple.

55
00:02:34,064 --> 00:02:37,014
After the war, money in Europe was weak.

56
00:02:37,054 --> 00:02:38,958
Buy coupons cheaply there.

57
00:02:38,998 --> 00:02:41,392
Cash them for more here.

58
00:02:42,442 --> 00:02:44,583
This usually takes forty five days

59
00:02:44,623 --> 00:02:47,076
according to Ponzi, who says his profits

60
00:02:47,116 --> 00:02:49,694
have reached four hundred per cent in some

61
00:02:49,734 --> 00:02:51,144
transactions.

62
00:02:51,794 --> 00:02:53,548
Four hundred per cent.

63
00:02:53,588 --> 00:02:56,729
It sounded like a loophole that bankers

64
00:02:56,769 --> 00:02:58,279
had somehow missed.

65
00:02:58,319 --> 00:03:01,133
There was just one way to check it.

66
00:03:01,173 --> 00:03:03,241
Count the coupons.

67
00:03:03,991 --> 00:03:06,860
Part two. He paid.

68
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:10,002
On July twenty sixth, the district

69
00:03:10,042 --> 00:03:11,256
attorney stepped in.

70
00:03:11,296 --> 00:03:13,639
A bar was placed today, on the rush of

71
00:03:13,679 --> 00:03:15,959
people to give their money to Charles

72
00:03:15,999 --> 00:03:18,342
Ponzi, head of the Securities Exchange

73
00:03:18,382 --> 00:03:20,662
Company, on his promise to repay with

74
00:03:20,702 --> 00:03:22,243
fifty per cent.

75
00:03:23,293 --> 00:03:25,371
Ponzi agreed to accept no further

76
00:03:25,411 --> 00:03:28,067
deposits, until an auditor selected by the

77
00:03:28,107 --> 00:03:30,250
district attorney has examined his

78
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:32,368
accounts, which are said to total

79
00:03:32,408 --> 00:03:33,586
millions.

80
00:03:34,936 --> 00:03:37,828
His offices were in the middle of Boston

81
00:03:37,868 --> 00:03:40,474
near City Hall, with an entrance on a

82
00:03:40,514 --> 00:03:42,548
narrow lane called Pie Alley.

83
00:03:42,588 --> 00:03:45,552
When the news broke, that lane filled with

84
00:03:45,592 --> 00:03:48,055
people who wanted their money back.

85
00:03:48,095 --> 00:03:51,269
Ponzi did something nobody expected.

86
00:03:52,319 --> 00:03:54,363
Money wizard pays all claims.

87
00:03:54,403 --> 00:03:56,727
Court denies injunction.

88
00:03:58,819 --> 00:04:01,841
Thousands of persons, in all stations of

89
00:04:01,881 --> 00:04:04,673
life, have invested money with Ponzi

90
00:04:04,713 --> 00:04:07,199
ranging from ten dollars to fifty

91
00:04:07,239 --> 00:04:10,108
thousand. The notes call for fifty per

92
00:04:10,148 --> 00:04:13,016
cent on their investment within ninety

93
00:04:13,056 --> 00:04:15,925
days. But in most instances, Ponzi has

94
00:04:15,965 --> 00:04:19,063
paid principal and profit in full in that

95
00:04:19,103 --> 00:04:20,086
time.

96
00:04:20,736 --> 00:04:23,096
He paid every claim, in cash.

97
00:04:23,136 --> 00:04:26,158
To the crowd, that looked like proof.

98
00:04:26,198 --> 00:04:29,634
A swindler runs. This man stayed and paid.

99
00:04:29,674 --> 00:04:33,171
So where was the money coming from?

100
00:04:33,921 --> 00:04:37,478
Part three. Count the coupons.

101
00:04:38,528 --> 00:04:40,431
Back to July thirty first.

102
00:04:40,471 --> 00:04:43,197
The wizard story jumps to page three

103
00:04:43,237 --> 00:04:45,631
under the same headline.

104
00:04:46,681 --> 00:04:49,202
A statement came from Postmaster Roland

105
00:04:49,242 --> 00:04:51,434
Baker, that the Boston post office

106
00:04:51,474 --> 00:04:53,864
redeemed only thirty dollars worth of

107
00:04:53,904 --> 00:04:56,622
international postal reply coupons, in the

108
00:04:56,662 --> 00:04:59,183
last three months, for Ponzi's exchange

109
00:04:59,223 --> 00:05:00,348
company.

110
00:05:01,398 --> 00:05:04,526
Thirty dollars. Against a fortune said to

111
00:05:04,566 --> 00:05:07,694
be nine million. If his profits came from

112
00:05:07,734 --> 00:05:10,630
reply coupons, those coupons had to be

113
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:13,489
cashed somewhere. In Boston, in three

114
00:05:13,529 --> 00:05:16,292
months, they barely existed.

115
00:05:17,342 --> 00:05:19,639
The federal attorney added the national

116
00:05:19,679 --> 00:05:21,855
figures. Up to and including the year

117
00:05:21,895 --> 00:05:24,312
ending June thirtieth, nineteen eighteen

118
00:05:24,352 --> 00:05:26,529
fifty four thousand, four hundred and

119
00:05:26,569 --> 00:05:28,925
thirty six dollars worth of coupons were

120
00:05:28,965 --> 00:05:31,243
redeemed by this government.

121
00:05:31,893 --> 00:05:34,786
Fifty four thousand dollars, in about ten

122
00:05:34,826 --> 00:05:36,861
years, for the whole country.

123
00:05:36,901 --> 00:05:39,723
Ponzi was said to be holding millions of

124
00:05:39,763 --> 00:05:41,082
the public's money.

125
00:05:41,122 --> 00:05:43,801
Whatever was paying his investors, the

126
00:05:43,841 --> 00:05:47,160
record says it was not postal coupons.

127
00:05:48,210 --> 00:05:51,076
But the dapper little man who has them all

128
00:05:51,116 --> 00:05:53,982
guessing, continues to smile, nonchalantly

129
00:05:54,022 --> 00:05:56,543
signing thousands of dollars worth of

130
00:05:56,583 --> 00:05:58,065
checks, and declaring

131
00:05:58,105 --> 00:05:59,743
I should worry.

132
00:06:00,493 --> 00:06:04,278
Part four. Hopelessly insolvent.

133
00:06:05,328 --> 00:06:08,522
August second. A new headline.

134
00:06:08,562 --> 00:06:11,965
Claims Ponzi is insolvent.

135
00:06:13,015 --> 00:06:15,998
Charges, that Ponzi is unbalanced on one

136
00:06:16,038 --> 00:06:19,097
subject, is hopelessly insolvent, has not

137
00:06:19,137 --> 00:06:22,045
sufficient funds to meet his notes, and

138
00:06:22,085 --> 00:06:25,143
has sent or received no money from Europe

139
00:06:25,183 --> 00:06:27,486
recently. Written by William H.

140
00:06:27,526 --> 00:06:30,283
McMasters, who has been employed as a

141
00:06:30,323 --> 00:06:32,661
publicity man by Ponzi.

142
00:06:33,311 --> 00:06:35,980
The accusation came from inside.

143
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:38,604
McMasters had worked for Ponzi.

144
00:06:38,644 --> 00:06:41,568
His article ran in the Boston Post.

145
00:06:41,608 --> 00:06:44,578
Ponzi answered the same day.

146
00:06:45,628 --> 00:06:48,601
McMasters' statement, that I have not sent

147
00:06:48,641 --> 00:06:51,399
money abroad, and that it has not taken

148
00:06:51,439 --> 00:06:54,412
the course of my system of operations, and

149
00:06:54,452 --> 00:06:56,923
returned to this country with heavy

150
00:06:56,963 --> 00:06:59,500
profits, is entirely false.

151
00:07:00,550 --> 00:07:02,687
The next day, the run got worse.

152
00:07:02,727 --> 00:07:04,524
Pays millions to investors.

153
00:07:04,564 --> 00:07:06,974
More than five hundred note holders

154
00:07:07,014 --> 00:07:09,763
anxious to get their money back, lined up

155
00:07:09,803 --> 00:07:11,872
outside the Pie Alley entrance.

156
00:07:11,912 --> 00:07:14,458
A line which extended out to City Hall

157
00:07:14,498 --> 00:07:17,683
Avenue, and was constantly increasing.

158
00:07:18,733 --> 00:07:21,159
A cheer went up from the waiting men and

159
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:23,895
women, when the doors were opened.

160
00:07:24,833 --> 00:07:27,446
A cheer, for the man they were lining up

161
00:07:27,486 --> 00:07:29,900
to leave. But the window was about to

162
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:30,938
close.

163
00:07:31,688 --> 00:07:34,680
Part five. Not one cent.

164
00:07:35,730 --> 00:07:39,850
August tenth. Ponzi sued as bankrupt, by

165
00:07:39,890 --> 00:07:41,530
creditors.

166
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:45,167
Charles Ponzi, financial wizard, announced

167
00:07:45,207 --> 00:07:47,545
tonight, that he would not pay out one

168
00:07:47,585 --> 00:07:50,562
cent of money tomorrow at his offices.

169
00:07:51,612 --> 00:07:54,128
Earlier in the day, involuntary bankruptcy

170
00:07:54,168 --> 00:07:56,502
proceedings had been instituted against

171
00:07:56,542 --> 00:07:58,024
Ponzi by three creditors.

172
00:07:58,064 --> 00:08:00,154
And the State Bank Commissioner had

173
00:08:00,194 --> 00:08:02,649
ordered the Hanover Trust Company, not to

174
00:08:02,689 --> 00:08:05,145
pay out any more money on checks of Ponzi

175
00:08:05,185 --> 00:08:06,637
or his agents.

176
00:08:07,687 --> 00:08:10,659
Three days later. Ponzi is arrested.

177
00:08:10,699 --> 00:08:12,667
Took in twenty millions.

178
00:08:12,707 --> 00:08:14,897
Says he cannot pay.

179
00:08:15,947 --> 00:08:18,485
Financial wizard said to be hopelessly

180
00:08:18,525 --> 00:08:20,860
insolvent, by officials who scanned

181
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:22,111
accounts.

182
00:08:23,161 --> 00:08:25,411
An estimate, that some thirty thousand

183
00:08:25,451 --> 00:08:27,942
persons entrusted upward of twenty million

184
00:08:27,982 --> 00:08:30,028
dollars to the promoter.

185
00:08:31,078 --> 00:08:33,811
And a post office inspector declared, that

186
00:08:33,851 --> 00:08:36,584
Ponzi's claim that he had made millions by

187
00:08:36,624 --> 00:08:39,225
manipulating international reply coupons

188
00:08:39,265 --> 00:08:40,744
was a stupendous fraud.

189
00:08:40,784 --> 00:08:43,385
And that in six years, dealings in those

190
00:08:43,425 --> 00:08:45,827
coupons, throughout the international

191
00:08:45,867 --> 00:08:48,336
postal union, would not add up to five

192
00:08:48,376 --> 00:08:50,627
hundred thousand dollars.

193
00:08:51,377 --> 00:08:55,324
Part six. Thus passes glory.

194
00:08:56,374 --> 00:08:58,734
November thirtieth, nineteen twenty.

195
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:01,134
Ponzi gets five year term for fraud.

196
00:09:01,174 --> 00:09:03,907
Promoter changes plea to guilty.

197
00:09:04,957 --> 00:09:07,591
It was charged that Ponzi had represented

198
00:09:07,631 --> 00:09:10,266
falsely, that he was able to pay interest

199
00:09:10,306 --> 00:09:12,745
at the rate of fifty per cent in forty

200
00:09:12,785 --> 00:09:14,767
five days, from profits made in

201
00:09:14,807 --> 00:09:17,690
international postal reply coupons.

202
00:09:18,740 --> 00:09:21,294
And the paper explained what the coupons

203
00:09:21,334 --> 00:09:23,628
had hidden. The little Italian, with

204
00:09:23,668 --> 00:09:25,898
prison sentences in Canada and this

205
00:09:25,938 --> 00:09:28,621
country behind him, developed his supposed

206
00:09:28,661 --> 00:09:31,086
scheme, only to have his house of easy

207
00:09:31,126 --> 00:09:33,679
money topple over on him, when the props

208
00:09:33,719 --> 00:09:36,978
of income from investors were pulled out.

209
00:09:38,028 --> 00:09:40,190
The props of income from investors.

210
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:41,826
That was the whole system.

211
00:09:41,866 --> 00:09:44,406
The money paid out to the early investors

212
00:09:44,446 --> 00:09:46,419
had come in from the later ones.

213
00:09:46,459 --> 00:09:48,685
When new money stopped, the payments

214
00:09:48,725 --> 00:09:49,828
stopped.

215
00:09:50,878 --> 00:09:54,457
As he faced the court, he wrote one line

216
00:09:54,497 --> 00:09:58,076
for the press. Sic transit gloria mundi.

217
00:09:58,116 --> 00:10:01,883
Thus passes the glory of the world.

218
00:10:03,233 --> 00:10:05,800
It was not the end of Charles Ponzi.

219
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:08,842
In nineteen thirty four he was deported to

220
00:10:08,882 --> 00:10:11,377
Italy. He died in Rio de Janeiro in

221
00:10:11,417 --> 00:10:14,129
nineteen forty nine, nearly penniless.

222
00:10:14,169 --> 00:10:17,170
The Boston Post won the Pulitzer Prize for

223
00:10:17,210 --> 00:10:19,560
public service, for exposing him.

224
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:22,602
And his name became the word for this kind

225
00:10:22,642 --> 00:10:23,966
of scheme.

226
00:10:24,716 --> 00:10:27,750
As printed: nine million dollars, from

227
00:10:27,790 --> 00:10:30,824
postal coupons. As recorded, under the

228
00:10:30,864 --> 00:10:33,898
same headline: thirty dollars worth of

229
00:10:33,938 --> 00:10:35,921
coupons, in three months.

230
00:10:35,961 --> 00:10:39,716
The answer was on page three all along.

231
00:10:40,766 --> 00:10:43,322
So how did he keep a city believing?

232
00:10:43,362 --> 00:10:46,278
He paid. Every payment looked like proof

233
00:10:46,318 --> 00:10:48,874
and every payment came from the next

234
00:10:48,914 --> 00:10:51,758
person in line. The thirty dollar figure

235
00:10:51,798 --> 00:10:54,138
was printed on July thirty first.

236
00:10:54,178 --> 00:10:56,805
Ponzi kept paying for nine more days.

237
00:10:56,845 --> 00:10:58,248
Here is my question.

238
00:10:58,288 --> 00:11:00,988
If you had been standing in Pie Alley

239
00:11:01,028 --> 00:11:03,872
holding one of his notes, would you have

240
00:11:03,912 --> 00:11:06,684
taken your money out, or waited for the

241
00:11:06,724 --> 00:11:10,208
fifty per cent? Tell me in the comments.

242
00:11:11,358 --> 00:11:14,189
If you like history told from the papers

243
